<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Low Level Concurrency on Chege's Blog</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/</link><description>Recent content in Low Level Concurrency on Chege's Blog</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><lastBuildDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Synchronization Primitives with Python Specifics</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/sync-primitives-w-python-specifics/</link><pubDate>Tue, 02 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/sync-primitives-w-python-specifics/</guid><description>&lt;div class="priors-holder"&gt;
 &lt;div class="priors" title="Priors: my guess before digging deeper"&gt;&lt;p&gt;In Chromium, why wasn&amp;rsquo;t explicit threading a thing? Data structures are usually
not thread-safe. There were guardrails for scenarios like checking &lt;code&gt;WeakPtr&lt;/code&gt;s
on a different thread. However, I didn&amp;rsquo;t come out of it with a mental model for
threading primitives.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;


&lt;h2 id="introduction"&gt;Introduction&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Threads are smaller units of a process that allow executing tasks in parallel,
sharing memory space. Threads are particularly useful for I/O bound tasks,
where much of the time is spent waiting for external resources. &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#threadingPy"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Coordinating Work Between Threads</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/coordinating-work-between-threads/</link><pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/coordinating-work-between-threads/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="the-problem"&gt;The Problem&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Some work should not be ran in the mainline as it takes too long, and makes your
mainline unresponsive, e.g., on user signup, sending a welcome email should be
done out of line lest your application cannot process new requests.
Conceptually, API handlers produce tasks, workers consume tasks, and something
sits in between to coordinate the handoff. &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#CoordinationHelloInterview"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Three things need solving in this scenario:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Efficient waiting&lt;/strong&gt;: Consumers should sleep when there&amp;rsquo;s no work, and wake
up immediately when new work arrives.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Backpressure&lt;/strong&gt;: Producers should slow down when consumers can&amp;rsquo;t keep up,
preventing memory exhaustion which would take down the whole application.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Thread safety&lt;/strong&gt;: The coordination mechanism itself must handle concurrent
access without corruption.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#CoordinationHelloInterview"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Ensuring Correctness in Concurrent Execution</title><link>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/correctness-in-concurrency/</link><pubDate>Thu, 22 Oct 2026 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://www.curiosities.dev/computer-science/programming-challenges/language-concepts/resource-management/low-level-concurrency/correctness-in-concurrency/</guid><description>&lt;h2 id="bug-patterns"&gt;Bug Patterns&lt;/h2&gt;
&lt;h3 id="check-then-act"&gt;Check-Then-Act&lt;/h3&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Involves checking a condition and then making a decision based on that check,
e.g., in a ticket booking system, checking that a seat is available and then
booking it if so. &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#HelloInterviewCorrectness"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Consider this buggy code:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;pre tabindex="0" class="chroma"&gt;&lt;code class="language-cs" data-lang="cs"&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;class&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nc"&gt;RateLimiterBroken&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;Dictionary&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;lt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_requestCounts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[];&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;private&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;readonly&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_maxRequests&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;100&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="kd"&gt;public&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;ShouldAllowRequest&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;string&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_requestCounts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;TryGetValue&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;out&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;&amp;gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_maxRequests&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;false&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;_requestCounts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;userId&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;count&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="m"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;true&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="line"&gt;&lt;span class="cl"&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;&amp;hellip; where thread A reads &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; as &lt;code&gt;99&lt;/code&gt;, but before it updates the map, thread
B also reads &lt;code&gt;count&lt;/code&gt; as &lt;code&gt;99&lt;/code&gt;. Both threads A and B increment the count from &lt;code&gt;99&lt;/code&gt;
to &lt;code&gt;100&lt;/code&gt; even though the user has made &lt;code&gt;101&lt;/code&gt; requests. &lt;span class="citation-ref"&gt;&lt;a href="#HelloInterviewCorrectness"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>